<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Leros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese-campaign">Dodecanese campaign</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Leros was fought in November 1943 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> island of Leros, which British and Italian troops had held since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s armistice in September. After weeks of air attacks, German paratroopers and seaborne forces landed on 12 November and forced the garrison to surrender four days later. It was one of the last major German victories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-leros.jpg" alt="Battle of Leros" width="320" height="502" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bauer · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-527-2348-21,_Kreta,_Fallschirmj%C3%A4ger_vor_Start_mit_Ju_52.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-cassibile">Armistice of Cassibile</a> on 8 September 1943, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> saw the Greek islands as a feasible objective, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> regarded operations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> as an unnecessary diversion from the Italian Campaign. German forces quickly took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and its three airfields, while British troops, the Special Boat Service and the Long Range Desert Group secured Kos, Leros and other islands. On 3 October the Germans landed on Kos, which fell the next day, depriving the British of air cover; on 4 October they shot Colonello Felice Leggio and 101 of his officers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>'s order to execute captured Italian officers.</p><p>Leros, Italian since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> and fortified around the deep-water port of Portolago, now Lakki, was held by about 7,600 Italians, most of them naval personnel, under Captain Luigi Mascherpa, and by about 3,000 British troops. Brigadier Robert Tilney, who took command on 5 November, replaced the plan to hold the high ground with a forward defence of the coast that spread his forces too thinly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 26 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a>, enjoying air superiority, bombed the island continuously, sinking the Greek destroyer Vasilissa Olga and HMS Intrepid in the harbour of Lakki on the first day. Early on 12 November German troops landed on the north-east coast and near the town of Leros, and in the afternoon about 600 paratroopers dropped over Mount Rachi, dividing the island in two. Italian batteries fought hard, and several captured Italian officers, among them Lt. Antonino Lo Presti, were executed.</p><p>British counter-attacks over the following days were sporadic and costly, and reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> could not turn the fighting; Lt Col Maurice French was killed in an attack that recaptured part of Apetiki. By the evening of 15 November the situation was hopeless. At 17:30 on 16 November Tilney surrendered, and Mascherpa, by then a rear admiral, followed at 22:00. About 3,200 British and 5,350 Italians were taken prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Germans lost 512 men killed or missing, the Italians 300 and the British 600, of whom 187 died on Leros and the rest at sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and the smaller islands were abandoned, and the Germans held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a> until the end of the war. Mascherpa was handed over to the Italian Social Republic, sentenced to death for defending Leros, and shot. The battle has been called the last great defeat of the British Army in the war, and many at the time criticised the operation as another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, blaming Churchill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Leros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Italy's armistice in 1943 German troops took over, defeating the British at <b>Leros</b> and deporting the Jews of Rhodes in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-islands-of-the-aegean">Italian Islands of the Aegean</a></li></ul></section>
